# dome - the dome over the rotunda: its shell, its ribs, its oculus and the lantern over it.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE PUTS UNDER LOAD: the hole. Everything else here is in service of it.
#
# The one image this building exists to produce is a shaft of sunlight falling through the oculus
# onto the rotunda floor. That is the hardest thing in the scene to render and the easiest thing in
# the scene to destroy by accident, because it is not made of geometry that anybody can see — it is
# made of geometry that is ABSENT, in a chain, all of it small:
#
#   the lantern lights   eight openings 0.45 m wide through a wall 0.135 m thick, and the ONLY
#                        way daylight gets in at all. Seal them and the rotunda goes black.
#   the well             a clear cylinder 1.80 m across and 1.61 m long, from the lantern floor
#                        down through the plinth to the top of the funnel
#   the funnel           the oculus splayed 1 in 2, 0.90 m radius at the top to 1.16 at the soffit
#   the room below       nine and a half metres across and sixteen and a half deep, lit through an
#                        aperture whose area is a fortieth of its floor. That ratio is the load:
#                        every photon in the rotunda has come through one 2.5 m^2 hole, so a path
#                        tracer that loses light here has nothing left to lose it from.
#
# DO NOT WIDEN THE LANTERN WALL AND DO NOT SHORTEN THE LANTERN LIGHTS. A ray that gets into the
# rotunda has to cross a 0.135 m wall between the outer face of the lantern and the well, and it
# drops tan(elevation) x 0.135 doing it. The lights are 0.72 m tall, so the lantern passes sun down
# to a shadow drop of 0.72 — an elevation of 79 degrees — and stops. Thicken the wall to a
# comfortable 0.30 and that ceiling falls to 67 degrees. The wall is thin because the light has to
# get past it, and the same is true of every one of these numbers.
#
# WHERE THE SHAFT ACTUALLY LANDS, with this engine's sun. The sun is fixed at (0.4, 0.85, 0.3) in
# src/app/main.cpp: 59.5 degrees of elevation, out of the east-north-east. At 59.5 degrees a ray
# through the oculus travels 0.588 m sideways for every metre it falls, and it has 16.40 m to fall
# from the oculus at 18.20 to the rotunda floor at 1.80 — so it moves 9.65 m sideways, and the
# rotunda is 4.80. THE DISC OF LIGHT LANDS ON THE ROTUNDA WALL, about eight metres up, and tracks
# down and across it as the day turns. That is not a fault in this part and it cannot be fixed from
# this file: it is what a 9.6 m room lit from 16.4 m above it does, and it is exactly what the
# Pantheon does for most of the year. For the disc to reach the floor the sun has to stand above
# 74 degrees, which this renderer's sun never does. `-Sun <elevation>` is asked for in
# clips/facility/requests/dome.md.
#
# The shaft ITSELF — the beam in the air — is the image, and it is real: photograph it with
# `--fog 0.03,0.95,0.7,200,0` and it is there, leaning north-east up to the oculus.
#
# The second load is the SURFACES THE LIGHT LANDS ON, which is why the materials are split the way
# they are: a 9.6 m concave plaster vault as the bounce surface, marble down the well where the
# beam grazes, and three different metals - copper on the skin, bronze on the oculus ring, gilt on
# the finial - so a path tracer is asked to tell polished, brushed and patinated apart in one frame
# against the sky. The copper is the only copper in the building.
#
# The third is thin matter in the ordinary way: eight colonnettes 0.135 m across (four voxels at
# the contract's metre 32) and twenty-four ribs 0.135 m wide standing 0.09 m proud of a curved
# surface, which is the sampler's problem, not the modeller's. Five welted cross-rolls cross them
# at 0.09 of relief on a 0.18 band — the ribs' width at two thirds their height — so the same
# curved surface is asked for two depths of fold at once.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DO NOT CHECK THIS PART WITH `--part part_dome` AGAINST clips/facility.clip. IT LIES.
#
# The manifest ends with `origin 0 -3.50 0`, and `apply_origin` moves the solid, the paint rules
# AND THE BOUNDS. It does not move the name `part_dome`, because a name is not part of the tree it
# was folded into. So that command samples an unshifted dome inside a box that has dropped 3.50 m:
# the box tops out at 16.50, the dome stands 15.45 to 19.60, and what comes back is the bottom
# 1.05 m of it with every paint rule 3.50 m below the stone. Reported: 11.750 x 1.000 x 11.750 m,
# ONE material, limestone. A twelve-metre saucer four fifths of a metre tall. Nothing is wrong with
# the dome; the box is in the wrong place, and the same arithmetic quietly beheads the drum and the
# parapet too.
#
# clips/facility/requests/cuts-probe-dome.clip is the same fragment in a box that has not moved.
# It reports 15.438 .. 19.594, radius 5.875, and the six materials this file paints.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHAT EACH CUT ACTUALLY REMOVES, measured by building the part twice — once with the cut and once
# without it — and differencing. The part itself is 85.02 m3 at metre 32, in ONE component with
# nothing floating.
#
#   dome_above     69.67 m3   (at metre 8) the lower half of both ellipsoids. Without it the part
#                             reaches down to y = 13.00: a bowl, not a dome. This is the one
#                             intersection here that is load-bearing rather than tidy.
#   dome_void     108.57 m3   (at metre 8) the inside. The shell it leaves is 0.90 at the haunch and
#                             0.45 at the crown, measured 0.533 m vertically at r = 3.0, which is
#                             the same thing said down a plumb line instead of along a normal.
#   dome_oculus     1.709 m3  the bore and the splay, through the crown. `--gap y@0,0` at metre 32
#                             reads 4.125 m of unbroken air up the axis, open at the bottom and
#                             closed at 19.30 by the corona: the hole is a hole all the way.
#   dome_lights     0.518 m3  eight openings through a 0.135 wall, 0.065 m3 apiece.
#   dome_lan_bore   0.843 m3  the well, hollowing the lantern drum from 18.25 to 19.30.
#   dome_rib_hub    0.0027 m3 EIGHTY-EIGHT VOXELS, and none at all at metre 8. See its own note.
#
# and the two things that are added rather than taken away, weighed the same way:
#
#   dome_ribs       2.818 m3  twenty-four standing seams
#   dome_welts      1.952 m3  five cross-rolls
#
# Nothing else in this file removes anything, and nothing in it is subtracted from another
# fragment: `part_dome` exposes no void and the manifest names none.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE MODULE. D = 0.90, M = 0.45, and everything across is a multiple or a simple fraction of one
# of them. Everything UP is counted in M/9 = 0.05, and that is not a licence, it is arithmetic:
# the contract fixes parapet_top 13.00, drum_top 15.60, dome_top 18.20 and lantern_top 19.60, and
# not one of those four is a whole number of modules while all four are whole numbers of M/9. So
# M/9 is the sub-module the top of this building is already built in, and every course in the
# lantern below is a multiple of it. The three heights that are not are named where they occur.
#
#   springing   15.60   drum_top: the dome starts where the drum stops
#   crown       18.20   dome_top, the outside of the shell at the centre line
#   finial      19.60   lantern_top
#   rise         2.60   18.20 - 15.60. It is also the height of the drum, 15.60 - 13.00.
#
#   intrados    4.80 wide, 2.15 high   4.80 is the rotunda's inner radius, from the contract.
#                                      2.15 is the rise less one module of crown thickness.
#   extrados    5.70 wide, 2.60 high   5.70 is 4.80 + D: the dome springs on a wall one diameter
#                                      thick, so the shell is D at the haunch and M at the crown,
#                                      which is the way a masonry dome is built and the reason it
#                                      does not need a chain.
#
# WHERE IT MEETS ITS NEIGHBOUR. The dome stands on an annulus of inner radius 4.80 and outer 5.70
# at y = 15.60, and its stonework reaches DOWN to 15.45 so it laps the drum by 0.15 whatever the
# drum turns out to be. Nothing in this file needs to know anything else about the drum, and
# nothing in the drum needs to read this file: 4.80 and 15.60 are both in the contract.
#
# The intrados is left plain. The coffers belong to the rotunda, which owns the inner dome; there
# is 0.90 m of shell at the haunch and 0.45 at the crown for its coffers to be cut out of, and its
# void is taken away in the manifest after everything is assembled, so they will cut mine too.
# THE ONE THING THAT MUST NOT HAPPEN is anything being built across the oculus. It is a hole.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE SHELL - two surfaces of revolution and the annulus between them
# ================================================================================================
#
# Both are ellipsoids on the springing line, so both stand vertically where they meet the drum and
# the wall below runs straight up into the dome without a kink. A sphere cannot do that at this
# rise: a spherical cap 2.60 high on a 5.70 half-span leaves the surface at 63 degrees to the
# vertical and the drum would appear to have been cut off.

let dome_ext_cap   = ellipsoid 0 15.60 0  rx=5.70 ry=2.60 rz=5.70
let dome_ext_skirt = cylinder  0 15.525 0 r=5.70 h=0.15 axis=y      # 15.45 .. 15.60, into the drum
let dome_hull      = union { dome_ext_cap dome_ext_skirt }

# The lower half of the ellipsoid is not a dome, it is a bowl. This is the knife.
let dome_above     = box -6.30 15.45 -6.30   6.30 18.65 6.30
let dome_outer     = intersection { dome_hull dome_above }

let dome_int_cap   = ellipsoid 0 15.60 0  rx=4.80 ry=2.15 rz=4.80
let dome_int_skirt = cylinder  0 15.375 0 r=4.80 h=0.45 axis=y      # 15.15 .. 15.60
let dome_void      = union { dome_int_cap dome_int_skirt }

# The roll at the foot, where the copper turns down over the drum's cornice. 0.18 (0.4 M) of tube
# on a 5.70 ring, so it stands out to 5.88 and covers the joint the ribs die into.
let dome_roll = torus 0 15.78 0 ring=5.70 tube=0.18 axis=y

# ================================================================================================
# THE RIBS - twenty-four, following the surface
# ================================================================================================
#
# A rib is the extrados grown 0.135 (0.3 M) outward and then sliced to a 0.18 (0.4 M) fin, which is
# the only construction that keeps it the same height all the way up: `offset` moves a surface
# along its own normal, so a rib built this way stands 0.135 proud at the haunch, at the crown, and
# everywhere between, and it never has to know the shape it is sitting on.
#
# TWENTY-FOUR AS SEVEN ROTATIONS MIRRORED TWICE, AND NOT AS `around { } count=24`. A polar fold
# reports a distance larger than the truth near a sector boundary, so the field must declare it
# infinitely slack, and infinite slack anywhere means the sampler cannot skip a single voxel of the
# bounds anywhere in the clip. Rotations are isometries: these are exact and each carries a box.
#
# THE ROTATIONS ARE NEGATIVE AND THAT IS NOT A TYPO. A positive turn about y in this engine carries
# +x toward -z. `mirror axis=z` asks its child at |z|, which is never negative, so a fin rotated
# into -z is asked about at a place it does not exist and VANISHES WITHOUT AN ERROR. Everything
# under a `mirror { mirror { } axis=x } axis=z` has to be built in the +x +z quadrant, which about
# y means turning the other way. Measured, not guessed: two fins at y=+0.125 gave two components,
# the same two at y=-0.125 gave six.
#
# `by=` IS NEGATIVE TO GROW THE SHAPE. `offset` ADDS its number to the distance, so a positive one
# pushes the surface inward and SHRINKS. Measured, not guessed: a sphere of radius 1 offset by
# +0.25 comes out 1.50 across and offset by -0.25 comes out 2.50. Written the intuitive way round
# the ribs were carved *into* the shell instead of standing on it, and since they were then unioned
# with the shell they simply disappeared — no error, no warning, a perfectly smooth dome.
let dome_rib_out  = offset { dome_hull } by=-0.135
let dome_rib_a    = box 0.90 15.45 -0.09   6.30 18.65 0.09
let dome_rib_b    = rotate { dome_rib_a } y=-0.0416667
let dome_rib_c    = rotate { dome_rib_a } y=-0.0833333
let dome_rib_d    = rotate { dome_rib_a } y=-0.125
let dome_rib_e    = rotate { dome_rib_a } y=-0.1666667
let dome_rib_f    = rotate { dome_rib_a } y=-0.2083333
let dome_rib_g    = rotate { dome_rib_a } y=-0.25
let dome_rib_quad = union { dome_rib_a dome_rib_b dome_rib_c dome_rib_d
                            dome_rib_e dome_rib_f dome_rib_g }
let dome_rib_fan  = mirror { mirror { dome_rib_quad } axis=x } axis=z

# Twenty-four ribs converging on a 1.80 m hole would be a mat, not a rib. They stop at 1.125
# (2.5 M), under the lantern's plinth, which is 1.26 across, so the last 0.135 of each is buried.
let dome_rib_hub  = cylinder 0 16.90 0 r=1.125 h=3.60 axis=y        # 15.10 .. 18.70
let dome_ribs     = difference { intersection { dome_rib_out dome_rib_fan dome_above } dome_rib_hub }

# THE HUB SUBTRACTION IS VERY NEARLY A GUARD, AND AT METRE 8 IT IS EXACTLY ONE. Measured, not
# assumed: the same part built with the `difference` and without it comes back at 42080 voxels BOTH
# TIMES at metre 8 — bit for bit the same clip — and at metre 32 the two differ by EIGHTY-EIGHT
# voxels, 2.7 litres, in a part of 2.79 million.
#
# The reason is that two other shapes have already taken almost everything inside r = 1.125: the
# oculus splay is a cone of radius 1.35 at y = 17.30 narrowing to 0.90 at the crown, so it is wider
# than the hub anywhere below y = 17.75, and the lantern plinth is a solid cylinder of r = 1.26
# from 17.90 to 18.25, which fills back in everything above. Between 17.75 and 17.90 there is a
# 0.15 m band where the cone has drawn inside 1.125 and the plinth has not yet arrived, and the
# ends of twenty-four ribs in that band are the eighty-eight voxels.
#
# It is kept, and it is kept knowingly: it is free, it is the only thing that would stop a rib
# reaching the axis if the oculus or the lantern ever moved, and it does in fact do a little work.
# What it must not be read as is a load-bearing cut, which is what its old comment implied.

# ================================================================================================
# THE WELTS — five copper seams across the bays, on the same construction as the ribs
# ================================================================================================
#
# A dome is not covered in one sheet of copper. It cannot be: the metal comes off a roll about a
# metre wide, and what a coppersmith does with it is lay it in bays between standing seams — which
# is what the twenty-four ribs are — and join one sheet to the next DOWN the bay with a welt across
# it. The ribs on their own are half the trade: 100 m2 of dead-smooth metal between them, which is
# the one thing on this building the eye reads as a rendering rather than as a roof.
#
# So: five rings at 2 D, 3 D, 4 D, 5 D and 6 D of radius — 1.80, 2.70, 3.60, 4.50, 5.40, a sheet
# apart in plan — each standing 0.09 (0.2 M) proud and 0.18 (0.4 M) across: the ribs' own width, at
# two thirds their relief, so the two read as the same fold of the same metal with the standing
# seam the taller of them, which is what they are.
#
# 0.09 AND NOT 0.0675, AND THE FIRST TRY WAS 0.0675. A welt half the ribs' height is the right
# drawing and it is the wrong solid: at 0.0675 proud on a 0.09 band the innermost ring, at r = 1.80
# where this dome is only eight degrees off flat, came back BROKEN — the part went from 9
# components to 57 and shed 104 voxels into the air, all of them in eighty-odd specks along that
# one ring, four-fold symmetric because the sampler's grid is. A ridge 2.2 voxels tall on a 2.9
# voxel band lying almost parallel to the voxel layers is not a ridge, it is a row of dashes. At
# 0.09 on 0.18 — 2.9 voxels on 5.8 — it is one ring, and the count went back to 9. Measured both
# ways; the arithmetic of it is the same arithmetic that made roof.clip take its fall in steps.
#
# BUILT THE RIBS' WAY AND FOR THE RIBS' REASON. `offset` moves a surface along its own normal, so
# a band cut out of the extrados grown by 0.0675 stands 0.0675 proud at the springing, at the
# crown and everywhere between, and never has to know what shape it is lying on. The cutter is a
# revolved section — an annular wall of vertical faces — because that is exact at every point in
# the world and a stack of tori would not be: a torus of ring 5.40 does not lie on this ellipsoid,
# it crosses it.
#
# `by=` IS NEGATIVE TO GROW, the same way round as the ribs above. Written the intuitive way the
# seams are carved INTO the copper instead of standing on it, they are then unioned with the shell,
# and the dome comes back exactly as smooth as it was before with no error anywhere.
#
# Nothing here needs cutting where a rib crosses it: a rib stands 0.135 proud and a welt 0.0675, so
# `union` swallows the welt into the rib for the 0.18 they share, which is what a welt dies into a
# standing seam looks like. And nothing here reaches the lantern: the innermost ring is at 1.80 and
# the plinth is 1.26.
let dome_welt_out = offset { dome_hull } by=-0.09
let dome_welt_sec = union {
    box 1.71 15.40 -1   1.89 19.00 1
    box 2.61 15.40 -1   2.79 19.00 1
    box 3.51 15.40 -1   3.69 19.00 1
    box 4.41 15.40 -1   4.59 19.00 1
    box 5.31 15.40 -1   5.49 19.00 1
}
let dome_welts = intersection {
    dome_welt_out
    revolve { dome_welt_sec } axis=y
    dome_above
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE OCULUS - 1.80 m (2 D) across, and it is a hole all the way through
# ================================================================================================
#
# A straight bore of radius 0.90 (2 M), and under it a cone that splays the soffit open at exactly
# one in two: radius 0.90 at 18.20, 1.125 (2.5 M) at 17.75, and it meets the intrados at about
# 1.16. The splay is what lets a low sun in at all, and it is what makes the underside read as a
# funnel of light rather than as a hole punched in a ceiling.
#
# The cone's numbers are all module: it stands on radius 1.35 (3 M) at y = 17.30 and comes to a
# point 2.70 (6 M) above that, at y = 20.00, which puts it at 0.90 exactly as it crosses the crown.
let dome_oc_bore  = cylinder 0 17.975 0 r=0.90 h=1.35 axis=y        # 17.30 .. 18.65
let dome_oc_splay = cone     0 17.30 0 r=1.35 h=2.70 axis=y
let dome_oculus   = union { dome_oc_bore dome_oc_splay }

# The bronze ring round the mouth of it, on the inside, where it is seen. Half buried in the
# soffit and hanging 0.06 below it: inner radius 1.17, which clears the splay's 1.16, so the ring
# frames the light and never stands in it.
let dome_rim = torus 0 17.70 0 ring=1.26 tube=0.09 axis=y

# ================================================================================================
# THE LANTERN - 18.20 to 19.60, and it is OPEN
# ================================================================================================
#
# Every course a multiple of M/9 = 0.05:
#
#   17.90 - 18.25   plinth        r 1.26  (2.8 M)   mostly buried in the crown; emerges at 18.14
#   18.25 - 19.20   drum          r 0.90 .. 1.035   the wall the eight lights are cut through
#   19.20 - 19.25   architrave    r 1.125 (2.5 M)
#   19.25 - 19.35   corona        r 1.17  (2.6 M)
#   19.35 - 19.45   cupola        rx 1.125, ry 0.15
#   19.60           the ball
#
# THERE IS NO STYLOBATE COURSE AND THAT IS ON PURPOSE. There was one, 0.10 tall, and it did two
# bad things: it put a second 0.135-wide ledge in the light path at 18.35, which costs a quarter of
# the sun that gets through the lantern at all, and it left the openings sitting in the middle of
# the wall so the drum read as a tin with holes punched in it rather than as an arcade. The
# arches now spring off the plinth, which is where an arcade this small springs from anyway.
#
# Nothing here oversails by more than 0.135 (0.3 M). The first pass gave it a 1.35 plinth and a
# 1.26 corona, and a lantern 2.70 across and 1.40 tall is not a lantern, it is a cake tin: the
# brim reads before the colonnade does and the whole thing looks like a lid somebody left on the
# dome. The height is the contract's and cannot move, so the width had to.
#
# THE WALL IS 0.135 THICK (0.3 M) AND THAT IS A LIGHT DIMENSION, NOT A STRUCTURAL ONE. See the
# note at the top of the file before touching it.
#
# The courses above the plinth each run 0.05 past their nominal line at both ends, into whichever
# neighbour is wider there, so no two of them meet on a bare shared face. A shared face would have
# held together as well — this is one union and `min` is continuous across it — but the manifest
# displaces the whole building by 12 mm at the end and 0.05 of lap is cheaper than finding out.
let dome_lan_plinth = cylinder 0 18.075 0 r=1.26  h=0.35 axis=y     # 17.90 .. 18.25
let dome_lan_drum   = cylinder 0 18.725 0 r=1.035 h=1.05 axis=y     # 18.20 .. 19.25
let dome_lan_arch   = cylinder 0 19.25  0 r=1.125 h=0.10 axis=y     # 19.20 .. 19.30
let dome_lan_corona = cylinder 0 19.30  0 r=1.17  h=0.10 axis=y     # 19.25 .. 19.35

# EIGHT COLONNETTES, engaged on the piers between the lights. The shaft is 0.135 across (0.3 M) -
# four voxels at the contract's metre - and half of it is in the wall, which is what lets a
# feature that thin be built at all without a mesher losing it: it is never a free-standing stick.
# At +-22.5 and +-67.5 degrees, so the eight lights fall on the axes and you can see straight
# through the lantern along the centre line of the building.
let dome_col_base  = cylinder 1.035 18.25  0 r=0.09   h=0.10 axis=y # 18.20 .. 18.30
let dome_col_shaft = cylinder 1.035 18.675 0 r=0.0675 h=0.85 axis=y # 18.25 .. 19.10
let dome_col_cap   = cylinder 1.035 19.15  0 r=0.09   h=0.20 axis=y # 19.05 .. 19.25
let dome_col_one   = union { dome_col_base dome_col_shaft dome_col_cap }
let dome_col_a     = rotate { dome_col_one } y=-0.0625
let dome_col_b     = rotate { dome_col_one } y=-0.1875
let dome_cols      = mirror { mirror { union { dome_col_a dome_col_b } } axis=x } axis=z

# THE EIGHT LIGHTS. 0.54 (1.2 M) wide, sill on the plinth at 18.25, round-headed on a 0.27 radius
# springing at 18.80, so the crown stands at 19.07 and there is 0.13 of drum left over it. Two
# thirds of the lantern's circumference is opening, and the openings are 0.82 of its 0.95. This is
# the aperture the whole building is lit through.
let dome_lig_body = box      0.72 18.25 -0.27   1.26 18.80 0.27
let dome_lig_head = cylinder 0.99 18.80 0  r=0.27 h=0.54 axis=x
let dome_lig_one  = union { dome_lig_body dome_lig_head }
let dome_lig_a    = rotate { dome_lig_one } y=-0.125
let dome_lig_b    = rotate { dome_lig_one } y=-0.25
let dome_lights   = mirror { mirror { union { dome_lig_one dome_lig_a dome_lig_b } } axis=x } axis=z

# The well: the shaft is 0.90 all the way from the top of the funnel to the cupola's soffit, with
# no ledge anywhere in it, because a ledge at r = 0.90 is a shelf the high sun lands on instead of
# the floor.
let dome_lan_bore = cylinder 0 18.775 0 r=0.90 h=1.05 axis=y        # 18.25 .. 19.30

# The rings get their arrises taken off together, which fillets where they meet each other as
# well. The colonnettes are left out of it on purpose: a 0.012 round is 18% of a 0.0675 shaft and
# would fatten the thinnest thing in this file by a fifth.
let dome_lan_rings = round { union { dome_lan_plinth dome_lan_drum
                                     dome_lan_arch dome_lan_corona } } by=0.012
let dome_lan_solid = union { dome_lan_rings dome_cols }

let dome_lan_cupola = intersection { ellipsoid 0 19.30 0 rx=1.125 ry=0.15 rz=1.125
                                     box -1.15 19.30 -1.15   1.15 19.45 1.15 }
let dome_lan_neck   = cylinder 0 19.475 0 r=0.045 h=0.15 axis=y     # 19.40 .. 19.55
let dome_lan_ball   = sphere   0 19.51 0 r=0.09                     # tops out at 19.60 exactly

# ================================================================================================
# ASSEMBLY
# ================================================================================================
#
# Everything solid, then everything hollow taken out of it in one go, and only then the three
# pieces that must not be hollowed: the ring hanging below the soffit, and the cap on the lantern.
#
# THE LIGHTS ARE ROUNDED BY 0.020 AND NOT BY 0.010, AND THAT ONE NUMBER IS WORTH EIGHT VOXELS AND
# A COMPONENT COUNT. A lantern light is a straight-sided opening cut through a round wall, so its
# jamb crosses the outer face at a glancing angle and the last sliver of pier before the opening is
# a knife edge. At metre 32 that edge shed ONE VOXEL at every one of the eight arch heads, at
# y = 18.91 and r = 1.03 — eight pieces of copper floating in the air inside the colonnade, which
# is what `components 9 (8 floating)` was, and which is invisible from anywhere a person stands.
#
# It is the lights and only the lights: built with them left out, this part comes back as ONE
# component with nothing floating, which is the control arm that settled it. Growing the opening by
# a further 0.010 moves the edge off the tangent and the whole part comes back as one piece. It
# also widens the aperture by 0.02, which is the one direction this file is allowed to move it.
let dome_mass = union { dome_outer dome_ribs dome_welts dome_roll dome_lan_solid }

let dome_hollow = union {
    dome_void
    dome_oculus
    dome_lan_bore
    round { dome_lights } by=0.020
}

let dome_body = difference { dome_mass dome_hollow }

let part_dome = union { dome_body dome_rim dome_lan_cupola dome_lan_neck dome_lan_ball }

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT
# ================================================================================================
#
# Every rule keys on a shape bound above in this file, and every one tests below=0.02 rather than
# below=0, because the manifest displaces the whole building by 12 mm at the end and a voxel that
# only exists because of that sits outside the shape that made it.

# Copper over the whole mass first; everything after this is an exception to it.
paint copper where=dome_mass below=0.02

# AND THE CROWNS OF THE WELTS, WHICH THE LINE ABOVE MISSES BY EXACTLY THEIR OWN HEIGHT.
#
# `union` is `min` and `intersection` is `max`, and `max` is an OVERESTIMATE outside the shape: a
# point 5 mm off a welt can be told it is 150 mm away, because the annulus that cut the welt out of
# the offset shell is the nearest of the three operands and it answers for the whole thing. So at a
# voxel on a welt crown the union's smallest honest number is `dome_outer`'s, and that is 0.09 —
# the welt's own relief — which is four times the 0.02 the rule tests. 728 voxels of the finest
# copper on this building came back LIMESTONE, a scatter of pale grit along five rings, and nothing
# in the report says so except a seventh material appearing in a list of six.
#
# The cure is a shape whose distance is honest where the welts are: the offset shell itself, whose
# zero surface passes exactly through their crowns. Every other outward face of the dome is 0.09
# inside it and is painted copper too, which it already was, and every exception below still comes
# after this and still wins.
# It takes both of these to clear it: the offset shell answers for the crowns, and the welts' own
# shape — tested loosely, at 0.12, because `max` is exactly what is being compensated for — answers
# for the shoulders where the band's vertical face meets the shell. 728 voxels of limestone, then
# 408, then none. Nothing within 0.12 of a welt is anything but copper, so a loose test here cannot
# reach anything it should not.
paint copper where=dome_welt_out below=0.02
paint copper where=dome_welts    below=0.12

# The patina, mottled. A copper roof is not one green. These key on the dome's own surface pushed
# about by the contract's shared grain, so the test only passes on voxels of MY shell - outside it
# the distance is large and positive and no amount of noise brings it under the threshold - and
# the pattern lands the same way on the drum's copper as on mine if the drum ever wants it.
let dome_patina = displace { dome_outer grain_broad } amount=0.18
let dome_bloom  = displace { dome_outer grain_fine  } amount=0.12
paint verde  where=dome_patina below=-0.11
paint bronze where=dome_bloom  below=-0.10

# The lantern is stone, not metal - only its cap is sheeted.
paint marble where=dome_lan_solid below=0.02

# The vault. Plaster, because this is the surface the shaft bounces off onto everything else in
# the rotunda, and it wants to be bright and matte rather than bright and shiny.
let dome_lining = offset { dome_void } by=-0.09      # negative grows it: see the rib note above
paint plaster where=dome_lining below=0.02

# The well and the funnel, in marble, because that is where the beam grazes the wall on its way
# down and a polished surface there is the difference between a shaft of light and a hole.
let dome_well = cylinder 0 18.45 0 r=1.0125 h=1.70 axis=y           # 17.60 .. 19.30
paint marble where=dome_well below=0.02

# The metals that are seen against something: the ring against the light coming through it, the
# ball against the sky.
paint bronze where=dome_rim below=0.02
paint copper where=dome_lan_cupola below=0.02
paint gilt   where=dome_lan_neck below=0.02
paint gilt   where=dome_lan_ball below=0.02

# THE CEILING OF THE WELL, LAST OF ALL, and it has to be last. It is the underside of the corona,
# and the cupola's paint reaches it — the cupola shape starts at 19.30 and the corona's soffit is
# at 19.30, so a coat keyed on the cupola covers the one surface at the top of the shaft that has
# to be bright. Looking up the oculus you saw a disc of green copper. This is a bright lid on a
# 1.61 m light well: every ray that comes in a south light and does not go straight down leaves by
# way of this, so it is the second most important 2.6 m2 in the building.
let dome_soffit = cylinder 0 19.275 0 r=0.99 h=0.15 axis=y          # 19.20 .. 19.35
paint marble where=dome_soffit below=0.02
